7] [17592186074738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1061 39871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cd88bb7-dfba-55b2-bb7a-0cce2188c27c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1061 39884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0ff59236-e8f5-575d-bc8b-731fac2b148a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1061 39885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "111068bf-412d-586d-b167-f6557ad36f93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074871 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074871 #edb/ref 1061 7777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074871 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "710f2d4c-d273-5b72-9ccc-f38b8242da5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074871 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074872 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074872 #edb/ref 1061 7778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074872 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ac944726-318f-5b1c-a830-c224ef6cc5c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074872 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074873 #edb/ref 1061 7780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074873 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "94e66fd6-c348-57bd-ad13-f242226deafe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074873 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074886 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074886 #edb/ref 1061 7928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074886 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08ccc6b4-9e8e-58ae-8c72-9c8924f4ac4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074886 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074887 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074887 #edb/ref 1061 7929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074887 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9ebb937-0b48-58ea-b518-557295155139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074887 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074888 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074888 #edb/ref 1061 7935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074888 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a54d3ef4-500e-59c3-ae05-4b3a53133012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074888 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074889 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074889 #edb/ref 1061 7942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074889 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0f0f170d-881d-5fd9-9bd7-874a2f2c72ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074889 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074890 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074890 #edb/ref 1061 7948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074890 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b3811aed-919d-5324-a2b3-37a2fc60a086" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074890 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186074974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074891 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074891 #edb/ref 1061 7949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074891 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc4b3a3b-3e46-5cab-95e5-4fe9b7a9b2d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074891 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074892 #edb/ref 1061 7955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "efe69386-b7fd-59d6-9a72-33a398873396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074966 #edb/ref 1064 "We can write ratios as fractions by placing the first number over the second number.\n\nSo, what ratio describes the number of rectangles to triangles below?\n\n![](images/25/25426db008d4b246f36959f51433e0a5cebe609fc01f9539d4f45af353919261.png)\n\nFirst, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $\\color{blue}4$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of triangles. We see that there are $\\color{red}3$ triangles.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}3}$. As a fraction, this is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nWe interpret this number to mean that there are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}4}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nrectangles for each triangle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074966 #edb/ref 1065 2047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d0b88e38-94c5-52f0-8b5d-de261fe7f82e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074966 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074972 #edb/ref 1064 "A **percentage** is another way of expressing a fraction that's used often in everyday situations. The word **percent** means \"out of $100$.\"\n\nLet's take a look at the square below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c2fd8e09cd4c0e7d6160c96c29b644c22e17c09d80e025e3cced2b391a20c94.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the square has been divided into $100$ equal parts, and\n- $\\color{blue}50$ of those parts are shaded.\n\nSince $\\color{blue}50$ out of $100$ parts are shaded, we say that \"$\\color{blue}50$ percent of the square is shaded.\"\n\nWe have a special symbol that we use instead of the word percent. This symbol is written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Large{\\%}\n$$\n\nUsing this symbol, we say that \"${\\color{blue}{5}0}\\%$ of the square is shaded.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074972 #edb/ref 1065 2071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074972 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1394fc74-71ad-5f1c-b032-940908e96849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074972 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074973 #edb/ref 1064 "When more than $100$ pieces are shaded in a model where $100$ pieces represent a whole, the percentage will be greater than $100\\%$.\n\nLet's take a look at the model below, where one large square represents one whole:\n\n![](images/5f/5fcb68226ada21bec7e2f4dd90fd5c9fde2c43578e1611e0faf85ddb580b5f34.png)\n\nIn this model, $170$ tiles are shaded. Therefore, this model represents $170\\%$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074973 #edb/ref 1065 2073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074973 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e1f95e0f-2d10-50e4-9cd1-a76c2fc35836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074973 #edb/ref 1067 "Percentages Greater Than 100%" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074974 #edb/ref 1064 "How do we find the percentage represented by a fraction model when the model isn't split into $100$ equal pieces?\n\nThe answer is to express the fraction as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the model below:\n\n![](images/17/17f55289981c45c7473462f06592a9577d692a9efba462b1101022ae8a630a4f.png)\n\nThis model represents the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTo make an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $100$, we multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $20$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} = \\dfrac{4 \\times 20}{5 \\times 20} = \\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{80}{100}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $80\\%$, we conclude that our fraction model represents $80\\%$.\n\nWe can also use equivalent fractions to construct a simplified fraction model for a given percent. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074974 #edb/ref 1065 2077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074974 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7140f82-2538-52a9-88ea-c84935add135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074974 #edb/ref 1067 "Representing Percentages Using Other Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nusing a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1065 6606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0baa395e-db83-5229-83f7-f40fd99e8a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075016 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7$.\n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n1 \\times 7 = 7\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1065 6608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5b799a98-0619-525c-b2da-433704f26bd8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075017 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Way of Computing a Product Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075037 #edb/ref 1064 "A **ratio** is a mathematical statement that expresses the relative sizes of two quantities.\n\nAs an example, let's look at the triangles and circles below.\n\n![](images/48/4865786ce55e0ec3fc1747ece4ea962425a3f83b2a45c7980f52719522318c82.png)\n\nWe can express the ratio of the number of circles to the number of triangles as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{\\textrm{number of circles}}}: {\\color{red}{\\textrm{number of triangles}}}\n$$\n\nTo find this ratio, we start by counting the number of occurrences of each shape:\n\n- First, we count the number of circles. There are $\\color{blue}5$ circles.\n- Next, we count the number of triangles. There are $\\color{red}2$ triangles.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$.\n\nWe can describe this ratio in words as follows:\n\n> *The ratio of circles to triangles is ${\\color{blue}5}:{\\color{red}2}$ because there are ${\\color{blue}5}$ circles*for every*${\\color{red}{2}}$ triangles.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075037 #edb/ref 1065 8277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075037 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3d8ebe22-8ef7-538a-a42d-4f1133689833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075037 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator.\n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions.\n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\nSince each part is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\nof a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5\n$$\n\nparts in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1065 8557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "577d7682-3ed8-5103-8641-0c02702a9e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1064 "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\nTo calculate\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\n, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10\n$$\n\npieces in total.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1065 8561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2de869cb-775d-5db4-b90d-7df0de218fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075059 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to help us to add fractions and whole numbers.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the value of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 + 1\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction. Let's solve this problem using a fraction model.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cd8994310039da418bd8983b9019c483931faca9fd6e49082f670f8a56f38.png)\n\nWe cannot add these two fractions at the moment, because they are split into different numbers of parts (i.e., they have different denominators). So what do we do?\n\nThe first shape is split into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts. So, we can split the whole number into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts also:\n\n![](images/08/086383f205c2725283ab00901e68681ec1b3e55aea2ae48639ec85519732c1ef.png)\n\nNow, both shapes are split into $\\color{red}2$ equal parts, and we have\n\n$$\n1+2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}\n$$\n\nshaded parts in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 2 + 1 = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer!\n\nLearning to add fractions and whole numbers will give us vital clues on how to add fractions with *unlike* denominators, which is our ultimate goal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075059 #edb/ref 1065 8985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075059 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "41682d46-db1d-506e-9bec-f61e2f4f67a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075059 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Prime Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087825 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "af9aa6af-5cbe-5933-9c7b-62e32a63386c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087826 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cd997467-85ee-5288-a574-2719cbff167b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying One as Neither Prime Nor Composite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087827 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b45eb1fc-7e00-5ece-8dcf-f27e6d4fb78e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1045 "Prime and Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087912 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "577ff41d-cd6b-5bfb-9a19-db4815d18bb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1061 40187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "61039992-084b-5a41-8a84-d5d32f71239e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088734 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1061 40188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dbf16eb4-9e74-5637-b88b-0c59bf07c9e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088735 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1061 40189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c44f455-2994-5b1f-b5a9-5ea9a33d7a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088736 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1061 40192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b769077a-6aa9-51b2-b700-b41bc8260115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088737 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1061 40196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03211f8f-c13f-570c-b8d1-d99bafd6d977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088738 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1061 40237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "758bd934-4f53-5080-bca6-e617305ccced" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a number is a **factor** of another number if it divides the number with no remainder.\n\nA whole number is a **prime number** if it has *exactly two* factors: itself and $1$.\n\nFor instance, $3$ is a prime number because it has only two factors, namely $3$ and $1{:}$\n\n$$\n3 = 3\\times 1\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many prime numbers. The first few prime numbers are\n\n$$\n2,\\quad 3,\\quad 5,\\quad 7,\\quad 11,\\quad 13,\\quad 17,\\quad 19,\\quad 23,\\quad 29,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nPrime numbers are very important. For example, computers use them to send and receive information across the internet securely." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1065 6639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee084b63-b5c9-54fe-8f73-750445535efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088985 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1064 "Numbers with *more* than two factors are called **composite numbers**.\n\nFor example, $4$ is composite because it has three factors, namely $1, 2$, and $4$.\n\nWe can think of composite numbers geometrically. Let the number $4$ be represented by a collection of four items:\n\n![](images/5b/5b0d7581cb6e9ce19bd9337ee1311ceb5260943b96ecd50984db3b3a03a5dd87.png)\n\nWe can pile the objects up to form a rectangle where the length and width are both greater than $1{:}$\n\n![](images/d6/d6d1f8ff0c888300090d1f3c438abcaed9f30e898f4cf7b5b2d6ccbbad27e831.png)\n\nIn contrast, a prime number of objects *cannot* be rearranged to form a rectangle where the length and width are *both* greater than $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1065 6640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db98b259-0f36-53b5-b62a-788af51d2b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088986 #edb/ref 1067 "Composite Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1064 "Every positive whole number is either prime or composite, but not both.\n\nThe only exception is the number $1$. The number $1$ is neither prime nor composite because it only has one whole number factor, namely $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1065 6646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2092df40-d7b5-5553-a2d1-05d8166f06bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088987 #edb/ref 1067 "One Is Neither Prime Nor Composite" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Segments and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105858 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d9e4943a-72d7-545e-b746-3a19ee6d3a83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Rays and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105859 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f50316d-ff00-53a1-af3a-fedd68e91f43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Lines and Their Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105925 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8db8b0ae-1a2a-546a-884d-bdf773c75483" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
